1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,000
CampusMarket — a campus second-hand trading platform (Track 1 · Database Systems)

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Live metrics from SQLite: 1,000 users · 5 categories · 10,000 products (3,961 available)

3
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Four pages: Marketplace · Product Detail & Purchase · Seller Management · My Purchases. Login is simulated by fixed test accounts.

4
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Marketplace — every query is parameterized SQL executed in the repository layer

5
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Keyword search — 140 available items match; the composite index seeks instead of scanning 10,000 rows

6
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Price-range filter (10–800 CNY) combined with the keyword

7
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Sort: price highest first — ORDER BY expressions come from a fixed whitelist

8
00:01:47,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Pagination via LIMIT/OFFSET; a separate COUNT(*) query computes the page count

9
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Seller management — publish, edit, and delete your own products (account #1)

10
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Publishing is validated by the service layer: title length, valid category, price > 0

11
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Published as product #10003 with default status "available" — one INSERT into the products table

12
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:11,000
My products — each row carries its status; sold rows keep buyer & finished_at and cannot be deleted

13
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Editing is ownership-checked too: UPDATE … WHERE product_id = ? AND seller_id = ? (covered by the test suite)

14
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:45,000
Switched to buyer account #2 — seller_id and buyer_id form two independent data lines

15
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:59,000
The buyer searches for the demo listing by title

16
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Product detail — seller, category, price, and current transaction state (available)

17
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Purchase = one conditional UPDATE … WHERE status='available' — exactly-once, decided by rowcount

18
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Revisiting the item: status 'sold', buyer #2, finished_at — a second purchase is rejected

19
00:04:46,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Query execution (real output): composite index vs forced full scan + EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN

20
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:17,000
13 automated tests pass: purchase semantics, 10 concurrent races, integrity rules, user journey

21
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Database internals: three tables, two indexes, zero foreign-key violations

22
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Three-layer architecture: Streamlit → services → repositories → connection factory → SQLite

23
00:05:47,000 --> 00:06:02,000
ER model: users publish & purchase products; categories group them; sale state lives on the product row

24
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Exactly-once purchases · layered design · verified by tests — thank you for watching
